rt_fmt_char_from_codepoint*¤Intrinsic boundary: encodes one Unicode scalar value as its UTF-8 string.
Args:
  cp: the scalar value to encode.
Returns:
  the UTF-8 encoding of `cp` as a string.:½
	encode_cp*«Encodes one Unicode scalar value as its UTF-8 string. Reuses the same
general codepoint-to-`str` primitive `std.fmt` already relies on for `{c}`
interpolation of a `char` -- not something introduced for casing, so
retiring it is out of scope the way `rt_str_to_upper`/`rt_str_to_lower`
(this module's actual replacement target) are not.
Args:
  cp: the scalar value to encode.
Returns:
  the UTF-8 encoding of `cp` as a string.:£
decode_utf8*Decodes `s`'s UTF-8 bytes into a list of Unicode scalar values.
Args:
  s: the string to decode.
Returns:
  the scalar values of `s`, in order.:Ó
lookup_one_to_one*¹Looks up `cp` in a sorted (key, value) table pair via binary search.
Args:
  keys: sorted scalar values, table keys.
  vals: mapped scalar values, parallel to `keys`.
  cp: the scalar value to look up.
Returns:
  the mapped value at `cp`'s index in `vals`, or @none when `cp` has no
  entry (i.e. maps to itself).:”
lookup_one_to_many*ùLooks up `cp` in a sorted 1:many table (up to 4 result scalars per entry,
`count[i]` of them meaningful).
Args:
  keys: sorted scalar values, table keys.
  counts: number of meaningful result scalars per entry, parallel to
    `keys`.
  cp0s: first result scalar per entry, parallel to `keys`.
  cp1s: second result scalar per entry, meaningful when `counts[i] > 1`.
  cp2s: third result scalar per entry, meaningful when `counts[i] > 2`.
  cp3s: fourth result scalar per entry, meaningful when `counts[i] > 3`.
  cp: the scalar value to look up.
Returns:
  the encoded UTF-8 string of the mapping, or @none when `cp` has no
  entry.:å
is_cased*ÔWhether `cp` is "cased" (General_Category Lu/Ll/Lt, or has a simple case
mapping) -- used only to evaluate the Final_Sigma condition below.
Args:
  cp: the scalar value to test.
Returns:
  true iff `cp` is cased.:–
is_case_ignorable*üWhether `cp` is case-ignorable (approximated as General_Category
Mn/Me/Cf) -- used only to skip over combining marks etc. when evaluating
the Final_Sigma condition below.
Args:
  cp: the scalar value to test.
Returns:
  true iff `cp` is case-ignorable.:´
	in_ranges*¢`los`/`his` are sorted, non-overlapping `[lo, hi]` inclusive ranges.
`partition_point` finds the first range whose low end exceeds `cp`; the
range just before it (if any) is the only one that could contain `cp`.
Args:
  los: sorted low ends of each range, table keys.
  his: high ends of each range, parallel to `los`.
  cp: the scalar value to test.
Returns:
  true iff `cp` falls within one of the `[lo, hi]` ranges.:²
is_final_sigma*›True iff a Greek capital sigma at `codepoints[i]` should lowercase to the
word-final form `Ï‚` rather than the ordinary `Ïƒ`: preceded by a cased
scalar (skipping any case-ignorable scalars in between) and not followed
by one.
Args:
  codepoints: the scalar values of the string being lowercased.
  i: the index of the Greek capital sigma within `codepoints`.
Returns:
  true iff the sigma at `i` is word-final.:¡
	map_upper*Full uppercase mapping of one scalar: the default multi-code-point
expansion if one exists (e.g. sharp s -> "SS"), else the simple 1:1
mapping, else the scalar unchanged.
Args:
  cp: the scalar value to map.
Returns:
  the UTF-8 encoding of `cp`'s full uppercase mapping.:Ç
map_lower_simple*®Full lowercase mapping of one scalar, *without* the Final_Sigma
exception -- callers that might be lowercasing a Greek capital sigma
(0x3A3) must check `is_final_sigma` first; see `to_lowercase` below.
Args:
  cp: the scalar value to map.
Returns:
  the UTF-8 encoding of `cp`'s full lowercase mapping.:
GREEK_CAPITAL_SIGMA	:
